-- Test Description
-- The purpose of this test is to ensure the proper functionality of the statistics 
-- registers.
--
-- Functional coverage																													        																																																								tion.
-- · Checks read & write functionality works for all statistic registers
-- · Checks counters increment to 0xff but do not roll over.
--
-- Detailed Test Description
-- 1. Initialise the GEM for 100-mode operation.
-- 2. Initiate reset.
-- 3. Check network control register and statistic registers for their proper start up value.
-- 4. Enable the statistic registers for writes (for test purposes).
-- 5. Write 0xaa, 0x55 and 0xff to all the statistic registers. Read all the status registers 
--    each time to ensure that the proper values are read back.
-- 6. Reset the statistic registers.  (Set bit 5 in the network status register)
-- 7. Read all of statistic registers to ensure they have the proper value.
-- 8. Write 0xfffd to the statistic registers. Then, increment all the statistic registers 
--    by one (Set bit 6 in the network control register to increment statistic registers).  
--    Read all the statistic registers to ensure that each register was incremented by one.
-- 9. Write 0xfff0 to the statistic registers.
-- 10. Write bit 6 in the network control register 18 times.  Therefore, the statistic 
--     registers should only increment up 0xffff or 15 times  (The registers should not roll over). 
--     Now, read all the statistic registers to ensure that they incremented properly.
-- 11. Read the statistic registers to ensure that all have been reset after the last 
--     register read. (After a read a statistic register should reset to zero)
-- 12  Test that snapshot registers are working properly.
-- 13. Set the Write Enable for Stastics Register bit in the Network Control Register. Then,
--     write 0xfffffffd to the octets transmitted lower register. Then, transmit a
--     frame. (This should cause the octets transmitted lower register to
--     roll over)
-- 14. Then, write 0xffff to the octets transmitted upper register and 0xfffffffd to the octets transmitted
--     lower register. Then, transmit a frame. (This should cause the octets transmitted upper register to 
--     hit 0x0000ffff  and octets transmitted lower register to hit 0xffffffff)
-- 15. Write 0xfffffffd to the octets received lower register. Then, receive a
--     frame. (This should cause the octets received lower register to
--     roll over)
-- 16. Then, write 0xffff to the octets received upper register and 0xfffffffd to the octets received
--     lower register. Then, receive a frame. (This should cause the octets received upper register to 
--     hit 0x0000ffff  and octets received lower register to hit 0xffffffff)
-- 17. Transmit a frame. Once the frame is totally transmitted, read the octets transmitted lower
--     register exactly when the register is going to be incremented and then read the octets
--     transmitted upper register.  Both registers should not be incremented. 
--     Ensures that the status registers can cope with being read at the same 
--     time as being incremented.
-- 18. Transmit a frame. While the frame is being transmitted, enable the take snap shot bit
--     in the network control register.  Then, set the read snapshot bit in the network control 
--     register once the frame is completely transmitted. Now, read the snap shot of the octets
--     transmitted lower register. Next, disable the the read snap shot bit in the network 
--     control register and read the octets transmitted lower register. (The count of octets 
--     transmitted lower register should have been incremented since the snap shot of the 
--     statistic registers was taken.)  Ensures that the stats registers can cope with being
--     read and reset at the  moment they are being incremented.  
-- 19. Receive a frame. Once the frame is totally received, read the octets received lower
--     register exactly when the register is going to be incremented and then read the octets
--     received upper register.  Both registers should not be incremented. 
--     Ensures that the status registers can cope with being read at the same 
--     time as being incremented.
-- 20. Receive a frame. While the frame is being received, enable the take snap shot bit
--     in the network control register.  Then, set the read snapshot bit in the network control 
--     register once the frame is completely received. Now, read the snap shot of the octets
--     received lower register. Next, disable the the read snap shot bit in the network 
--     control register and read the octets received lower register. (The count of octets 
--     received lower register should have been incremented since the snap shot of the 
--     statistic registers was taken.)  Ensures that the stats registers can cope with being
--     read and reset at the  moment they are being incremented.  
-- 21. Ensure that all bits of snapshot are toggled for codecoverage by writing all one's
--     to the incrementing statistics registers and then taking a snapshot.
--
